# This working tree travels between machines on one external drive, so the usual
# per-machine core.autocrlf setting cannot help: the files move, they are not checked
# out again on the other side, and whatever bytes are on the drive stay on the drive.
# The line ending is therefore pinned in the repository itself. Both platforms write
# LF into the working tree and the tree stays byte-identical wherever it is plugged in.
#
# This also closes for good a trap that has already cost this project twice: a shell
# script that silently acquires CRLF stops working inside the Docker image, because the
# carriage return becomes part of the shebang.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The vendored tiktoken BPE cache file must be treated as binary. Its filename is a sha1 hash
# that tiktoken looks up directly, and its contents are checked against a hardcoded sha256 hash
# at load time (see tiktoken/load.py). Line-ending normalization (LF -> CRLF on Windows checkout)
# would silently corrupt it and break that integrity check.
src/trimitdown/tiktoken_cache/* binary
